[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf` returns `EOF` on input failure and `0` on matching failure; in both cases
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m it assigns nothing, so `x` keeps the `= 0` from its declaration and the program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m prints `300` rather than reporting an error. Verified for: empty stdin, `/dev/null`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m closed stdin, whitespace-only input, non-numeric first character, and a sign with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m no digits after it (`-`, `+`, `- 5`, `--5`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A translation that treated a parse failure as an error would print nothing and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exit non-zero here. The Rust code returns `None` from `scan_int` and leaves `x`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m untouched, matching.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. `%d` skips whitespace across newlines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Directive whitespace is unbounded and includes `\n`, `\t`, `\r`, `\v`, `\f`, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"\n\n\t 7\n"` yields 7, not a failure. This is the `scanf`-vs-`fgets`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m distinction: a line-oriented translation would have failed on leading blank
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m lines. Verified up to 70,000 leading newlines.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. The conversion stops at the first byte that cannot extend the number
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `12abc` → 12, `12.75` → 12, `0x10` → 0, `1e3` → 1, `1,000` → 1, `5\0` → 5.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Leading zeros are decimal, not octal: `007` → 7, `010` → 10.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 4. Out-of-range magnitudes clamp to `long`, then truncate to `int`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m glibc parses `%d` into a `long` (clamping to `LONG_MAX` / `LONG_MIN` on overflow)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and stores it through the `int *`, keeping the low 32 bits. So:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | input | long value | stored `int` | printed |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `9223372036854775807` (`LONG_MAX`) | `LONG_MAX` | `-1` | `298` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `9223372036854775808` (clamped) | `LONG_MAX` | `-1` | `298` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `99999999999999999999` (clamped) | `LONG_MAX` | `-1` | `298` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-9223372036854775808` (`LONG_MIN`) | `LONG_MIN` | `0` | `300` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-99999999999999999999` (clamped) | `LONG_MIN` | `0` | `300` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4294967296` (2^32) | `4294967296` | `0` | `300` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `3000000000` | `3000000000` | `-1294967296` | `1705033004` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two distinct wrong answers were possible here and both are avoided: saturating to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `i32::MAX`/`i32::MIN` instead of truncating, and detecting overflow at 32 bits
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m instead of at 64. The Rust `scan_int` accumulates in `u128`, saturates at the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `long` boundary, then casts `i64 as i32`. Note this is glibc/LP64-specific
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m behavior, which is what the C ground truth exhibits on this platform.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 5. `2*x + 300` is wrapping 32-bit signed arithmetic
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `INT_MAX` → `2*x` wraps to `-2`, printed `298`. `INT_MIN` → `2*x` wraps to `0`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m printed `300`. `2^30` → `2*x` is exactly `INT_MIN`, printed `-2147483348`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A debug-profile Rust build using plain `*` and `+` would panic with "attempt to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m multiply with overflow" where the C wraps; `wrapping_mul` / `wrapping_add` are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m used instead, and the suite is run in the debug profile precisely to catch that.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 6. Output formatting
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printf("%d\n", y)` — decimal, no padding, single trailing newline, nothing else
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m on stdout. The Rust `write!(out, "{}\n", y)` matches byte for byte, including for
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m negative results.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Input classes covered by the suite
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Empty; `/dev/null`; closed stdin; each whitespace character alone and combined;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 70k-newline prefix; every one of the 256 possible leading bytes; non-numeric
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m leads (`abc`, `.5`, `e5`, `,5`, `\0`, `\xff`); lone and repeated signs; single
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m digit; small values around the `2*x+300 == 0` point (`-150`, `-151`, `149`); no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m trailing newline; `-0`; leading-zero padding (20 and 4096 zeros); digits followed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m by non-digits; multiple numbers on one line; `INT_MAX`/`INT_MIN` and their
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m neighbours; `2^30`, `1073741674` (result lands exactly on `INT_MIN`); values
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m between 2^31 and 2^64; `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` ±1; magnitudes far past 2^64;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 100,000-digit runs (positive and negative); and a sweep of `±(2^k − 1, 2^k, 2^k + 1)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m for k = 0..63.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
