The four behaviors where a naive translation would have diverged, all confirmed correct in the existing Rust:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- A failed conversion assigns nothing, so [38;5;10mx[0m stays 0 and the program prints [38;5;10m300[0m and exits 0 — for empty stdin, whitespace-only input, closed stdin, and a lone sign. A translation treating this as an error would exit non-zero.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10m%d[0m skips whitespace including newlines, so [38;5;10m"\n\n\t 7"[0m yields 7 rather than failing.[0m[0m
- Out-of-range input clamps to [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m and is then stored through an [38;5;10mint *[0m, keeping the low 32 bits: [38;5;10m99999999999999999999[0m prints [38;5;10m298[0m, not a saturated [38;5;10mi32[0m result. Saturating at 32 bits instead of 64 would have been wrong twice over.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10m2*x + 300[0m wraps as 32-bit signed arithmetic. This is why the suite runs in the debug profile too: plain [38;5;10m*[0m would panic on [38;5;10mINT_MAX[0m where the C wraps.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Coverage includes all 256 possible leading bytes, [38;5;10mLONG_MAX[0m/[38;5;10mLONG_MIN[0m ±1, values between 2^31 and 2^64, 100,000-digit runs, a 70,000-newline prefix, and a sweep of [38;5;10m±(2^k−1, 2^k, 2^k+1)[0m for k = 0..63. I also ran an ad-hoc randomized fuzz of ~3000 inputs outside the suite, with zero divergences.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
One caveat worth flagging: the clamp-then-truncate behavior is glibc- and LP64-specific. The Rust hardcodes 64-bit [38;5;10mlong[0m semantics, which matches the C as built here, but the two would diverge on a platform where [38;5;10mlong[0m is 32 bits.[0m[0m
